# Where npm gets @testagentic/atf: a private package on the public npm registry, readable with the access token you
# were given. This file is committed: never write the token itself here.
#
# Set ATF_NPM_TOKEN in your environment, and as a secret in your CI (the Copilot cloud agent's setup steps read the
# repository's Agents secret ATF_NPM_TOKEN). npm reads it when it runs; nothing stores it.
#
# The scope line keeps @testagentic on registry.npmjs.org even when your npm config sends everything else to another
# registry (a company mirror, for example). The token line is written ${ATF_NPM_TOKEN?}: with the variable unset, npm
# reads an empty token rather than the literal text, so every other npm command still works and only the framework's
# own install is refused (https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v11/configuring-npm/npmrc, "Environment variables can be replaced
# using ${VARIABLE_NAME} ... By adding ? after variable name they can be forced to evaluate to an empty string instead").
@testagentic:registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/
//registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken=${ATF_NPM_TOKEN?}
